From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Charlie Shao <charlie.shao@nordicsemi.no>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:55:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(zephyr): keep a stalled periodic upload from freezing the
 application

The periodic-upload work queue is started at
K_HIGHEST_APPLICATION_THREAD_PRIO, which outranks every thread this
application owns -- the HTTPS and MQTT clients at priority 5, and the
log processing thread at K_LOWEST_APPLICATION_THREAD_PRIO. Any upload
that stops yielding therefore takes the whole application down with it,
UART logging included, and the previous commit's tls_heap_lock wrap
means it does so while holding the project-wide TLS mutex.

The response-wait loop that could spin forever is fixed separately, but
the priority and the unbounded K_FOREVER lock wait are what turned that
single bug into an unrecoverable freeze. Remove both amplifiers so a
future stall in this path degrades Memfault uploads only:

- Run the queue below the application's own network threads. This is
  background maintenance; it has no reason to preempt anything.
- Bound the lock wait and skip the cycle if the mutex is not available.
  The next timer expiry retries, and the data is still queued in the
  packetizer, so nothing is lost.
---
 .../zephyr/common/memfault_http_periodic_upload.c   | 18 ++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_http_periodic_upload.c b/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_http_periodic_upload.c
index d6d04de..b1f3a21 100644
--- a/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_http_periodic_upload.c
+++ b/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_http_periodic_upload.c
@@ -30,6 +30,13 @@
 // corrupt the shared heap even after fixing the post_data() socket leak.
 #include "tls_heap_lock.h"
 
+// Background maintenance: keep this queue below the application's own network
+// threads (priority 5) and well above the log processing thread, so a stall
+// here can never starve the rest of the system. The SDK default of
+// K_HIGHEST_APPLICATION_THREAD_PRIO froze the whole application, logging
+// included, whenever an upload stopped yielding.
+#define MEMFAULT_HTTP_WORK_Q_PRIORITY 7
+
 #if CONFIG_MEMFAULT_HTTP_PERIODIC_UPLOAD_USE_DEDICATED_WORKQUEUE
 static K_THREAD_STACK_DEFINE(
     memfault_http_stack_area, CONFIG_MEMFAULT_HTTP_DEDICATED_WORKQUEUE_STACK_SIZE);
@@ -42,7 +49,14 @@ static void prv_metrics_work_handler(struct k_work *work) {
   }
 
   MEMFAULT_LOG_DEBUG("POSTing Memfault Data");
-  k_mutex_lock(&tls_heap_lock, K_FOREVER);
+  // Bounded: the queued data stays in the packetizer, so skipping this cycle
+  // and letting the next timer expiry retry is always safe, whereas waiting
+  // K_FOREVER on a holder that never releases is not recoverable.
+  if (k_mutex_lock(&tls_heap_lock, TLS_HEAP_LOCK_TIMEOUT) != 0) {
+    MEMFAULT_LOG_WARN("TLS heap busy, skipping this upload");
+    return;
+  }
+
   memfault_zephyr_port_post_data();
   k_mutex_unlock(&tls_heap_lock);
 }
@@ -80,7 +94,7 @@ static int prv_background_upload_init() {
 
   k_work_queue_start(&memfault_http_work_q, memfault_http_stack_area,
                      K_THREAD_STACK_SIZEOF(memfault_http_stack_area),
-                     K_HIGHEST_APPLICATION_THREAD_PRIO, &config);
+                     MEMFAULT_HTTP_WORK_Q_PRIORITY, &config);
 #endif
   return 0;
 }
